Wed Mar  3 10:39:52 EST 2010

We can run in a mode where the primary interrupt routine essentially
'backgrounds' the DMA transfers from the FADC module rather than waiting for
them to complete.  This frees up the CPU on the the V6100, allowing it to do
other tasks in parallel.

Requirements:
  - 'special' kernel:  
    /site/coda/kern/5.5/vx6100_v4.1veat
  - use the updated mollerDmaLib, mollerFadcLib, flexIOlib
    - mollerFADClib 
      /site/coda/2.6/extensions/fadc/mollerDmaLib/
      /site/coda/2.6/extensions/fadc/v1.2/
    - see boot script example here:
      /site/coda/2.6/extensions/fadc/mollerDmaLib/roc6_coda261_async.boot
  - The readout list (CRL) is structured a little differently:
    - CRL example:  
      /site/coda/2.6/extensions/fadc/mollerDmaLib/gen_list.crl
      - The nominal CRL is very simple.  It basically just pushes data around
        in some internal buffers and does some internal bookkeeping.
    - The heavy lifting, and bulk of the user code, is here:
      /site/coda/2.6/extensions/fadc/mollerDmaLib/mollerUserLib.c
      - Each CRL function (Go, End, Prestart, Trigger, etc...) has a twin here:
        vmeUserEnd(), vmeUserPrestart(), ...
      - The vmeUserXXXXX() functions are structure basically the same as you
        would write the usual CRL code.  Note, however, the additional code
        supporting the background DMA buffer handling, DMA status checking, and
        the DMA hand-off in the vmeUserTrigger().
      - *dma_bufp plays the role of *rol_bufp



NOTES:
- The development was done with the coda v2.6 ROC codebase.  It is not clear if
  the v2.5 ROC binary will work. (It should, but we should watch for problems.)

- The FADC DMA transfer should be the last thing that is done when handling
  module readout in mollerUserLib.c (so read out other modules, FADC scalers
  data, etc first).

- We are not able to flush all of the data in the internal buffers at the end
  of run (should only be a few events).   For the Moller DAQ application, this
  does not matter.  It is just as if the run ended a few events before the user
  clicked 'End'.

